even just a few months ago this site  would have seemed extraordinary  a masked prime minister arriving at  westminster abbey to be welcomed by  masked military  a masked clergy but as the trappings of  the pandemic become ever more familiar  the government is concerned compliance  with restrictions is diminishing  and new national measures may be needed  the nation faces a  tipping point and we have a choice  and the choice is either that everybody  follows the rules  the rule of six and the need to  self-isolate if you have a positive test  or if you're contacted by  nhs test and trace or  we will have to take more measures  but the labour leader says the  government should be admitting that its  own failings have contributed to the  current situation  rather than blaming the public because  the government's now  effectively lost control of testing it  doesn't necessarily know where the virus  is so you know if i was the prime  minister i would  apologize for the fact that testing is  all over the place and instead of using  the summer to  prepare for the autumn which is what we  said should happen uh we're in this  position just when we need testing to be  at its very best ministers have spent  the weekend examining the options  available for new measures  including what's been described as a  two-week circuit breaker lockdown  which could see pubs restaurants and  leisure activities closed for a  fortnight  to curb the spike in infections but one  leading scientist told sky news such  action could be jumping the gun  and should instead be used at a point  when the seasonal prevalence of the  virus is likely to be more dangerous  often when we come back in january  there's a significant rise in deaths in  the month of january  about 5000 per week more than what we  see  right now and that's due to the seasonal  effects  therefore if you're looking at a break  and when we need it we need it in the  mid-winter  when we might run into problems but  actually it would be more explainable to  people why we're doing it then  than now there's no evidence right now  of what's called a second wave  these are high pressure decisions for  the prime minister what he does this  week could define his political future  boris johnson has set his stall on  preventing another full  national lockdown and so to avoid  failure by his own metrics  he needs any new measures that are  introduced by the government to be  effective and to bring case rates down  but that could all be hampered  by his own back benches some of whom  feel that the existing regulations go  too far  and they may yet mount a rebellion in  parliament  the task for the government is to stem  the rise of infections  while keeping the public and the  conservative party on board  missteps or indecision this week could  result  in the opposite tom rayner sky news  westminster
 

